2026-10-28T17:39:57.8764598Z {"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01MBwrnN6s5cXHgrM3d3b8Yn","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t{\n2\t  \"number\": 28,\n3\t  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/28\",\n4\t  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n5\t  \"title\": \"[Bug]: aws_bedrockagentcore_harness: service-populated memory retrieval_config makes every apply fail with \\\"inconsistent result after apply\\\"\",\n6\t  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform v1.14.3\\non darwin_arm64\\n+ provider registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws v6.60.0\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n* `aws_bedrockagentcore_harness`\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `memory.agentcore_memory_configuration` only references an existing AgentCore Memory by ARN and declares no `retrieval_config` block, apply should succeed and the following plan should be empty.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nEvery apply that creates or replaces the harness fails with `Provider produced inconsistent result after apply`, because the service returns one `retrieval_config` entry per memory strategy and the provider writes them into a block the configuration never declared:\\n\\n```\\n.memory[0].agentcore_memory_configuration[0].retrieval_config:\\nblock count changed from 0 to 2.\\n```\\n\\nThe harness is created in AWS, but the resource is left tainted, so the next apply replaces it and fails the same way. I hit this 5 times out of 5.\\n\\nTwo further points:\\n\\n1. `retrieval_config` carries `listvalidator.SizeAtMost(1)`, so the state that the provider itself writes (2 entries) could not be expressed in a configuration.\\n2. The order of the two entries is not stable across applies. In the SDK, `RetrievalConfig` is a `map[string]HarnessAgentCoreMemoryRetrievalConfig` keyed by namespace path template, so there is no order to preserve, but the schema models it as a list and the flatten path iterates `MapKeys()` without sorting. Watching the state across consecutive applies of an unchanged configuration:\\n\\n   | apply | `retrieval_config` order (`map_block_key` / strategy) |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | 1 | `/{sessionId}` (summarization), `default` (semantic) |\\n   | 2 | `/{sessionId}` (summarization), `default` (semantic) |\\n   | 3 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n   | 4 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n   | 5 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n\\n   Because the block is modelled as a list, this ordering is part of the value being compared, so it can also surface as `.retrieval_config[0].strategy_id: was cty.StringVal(\\\"<semantic id>\\\"), but now cty.StringVal(\\\"<summarization id>\\\")` rather than as a block count change. We see that variant on harnesses that carry `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [memory] }`, where the count mismatch is suppressed but `memory_actual` still moves.\\n\\n### Relevant Error/Panic Output\\n\\n```console\\nError: Provider produced inconsistent result after apply\\n\\nWhen applying changes to aws_bedrockagentcore_harness.test, provider\\n\\\"provider[\\\\\\\"registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws\\\\\\\"]\\\" produced an unexpected\\nnew value: .memory[0].agentcore_memory_configuration[0].retrieval_config:\\nblock count changed from 0 to 2.\\n\\nThis is a bug in the provider, which should be reported in the provider's own\\nissue tracker.\\n```\\n\\n### Sample Terraform Configuration\\n\\n<details open>\\n<summary>Click to expand configuration</summary>\\n\\n```hcl\\nterraform {\\n  required_providers {\\n    aws = {\\n      source  = \\\"hashicorp/aws\\\"\\n      version = \\\"6.60.0\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nprovider \\\"aws\\\" {\\n  region = \\\"us-west-2\\\"\\n}\\n\\ndata \\\"aws_iam_policy_document\\\" \\\"assume_role\\\" {\\n  statement {\\n    effect  = \\\"Allow\\\"\\n    actions = [\\\"sts:AssumeRole\\\"]\\n\\n    principals {\\n      type        = \\\"Service\\\"\\n      identifiers = [\\\"bedrock-agentcore.amazonaws.com\\\"]\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_iam_role\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name               = \\\"harness-retrieval-config-repro\\\"\\n  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.assume_role.json\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_iam_role_policy\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  role = aws_iam_role.test.name\\n\\n  policy = jsonencode({\\n    Version = \\\"2012-10-17\\\"\\n    Statement = [{\\n      Effect   = \\\"Allow\\\"\\n      Action   = [\\\"bedrock:InvokeModel\\\", \\\"bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream\\\"]\\n      Resource = \\\"*\\\"\\n    }]\\n  })\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name                  = \\\"harness_repro_mem\\\"\\n  event_expiry_duration = 7\\n}\\n\\n# Two strategies on the same memory. This is what makes the service return\\n# two retrieval configs to the harness.\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy\\\" \\\"semantic\\\" {\\n  name                = \\\"harness_repro_semantic\\\"\\n  memory_id           = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.id\\n  type                = \\\"SEMANTIC\\\"\\n  namespace_templates = [\\\"default\\\"]\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy\\\" \\\"summarization\\\" {\\n  name                = \\\"harness_repro_summary\\\"\\n  memory_id           = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.id\\n  type                = \\\"SUMMARIZATION\\\"\\n  namespace_templates = [\\\"/{sessionId}\\\"]\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_harness\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  harness_name       = \\\"harness_retrieval_config_repro\\\"\\n  execution_role_arn = aws_iam_role.test.arn\\n\\n  # No retrieval_config block: everything that ends up in state under\\n  # retrieval_config comes from the service.\\n  memory {\\n    agentcore_memory_configuration {\\n      arn = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.arn\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  model {\\n    bedrock_model_config {\\n      model_id = \\\"anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-20250514\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  system_prompt {\\n    text = \\\"You are a helpful assistant.\\\"\\n  }\\n\\n  depends_on = [\\n    aws_iam_role_policy.test,\\n    aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy.semantic,\\n    aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy.summarization,\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n### Steps to Reproduce\\n\\n1. `terraform apply`. The harness is created in AWS, but the apply exits non-zero with the error above.\\n2. `terraform apply` again. The plan now says `is tainted, so must be replaced`, and the replacement fails the same way. Repeating this never converges.\\n3. `terraform show -json` after each attempt to observe the two `retrieval_config` entries the service added, and their changing order.\\n\\nThe state can be recovered with `terraform untaint aws_bedrockagentcore_harness.test` plus `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [memory] }`, after which plans are empty. That only hides the diff, and the first create still fails.\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\nn/a. Happy to capture `TF_LOG=DEBUG` output if it would help.\\n\\n### GenAI / LLM Assisted Development\\n\\nClaude Code was used to research the provider source and to draft this report. The configuration above was applied against AWS, and the error output and the ordering table come from those runs.\\n\\n### Important Facts and References\\n\\n* Schema (`internal/service/bedrockagentcore/harness.go`): `retrieval_config` is a `ListNestedBlock` with `listvalidator.SizeAtMost(1)`, and none of `map_block_key`, `relevance_score`, `strategy_id`, `top_k` are `Computed`. The service fills all four (it returned `relevance_score = 0.2` and `top_k = 10` for both entries here), so with a memory that has two strategies the provider writes a value the schema does not allow to be written by hand.\\n* Underlying API shape: `HarnessAgentCoreMemoryConfiguration.RetrievalConfig` is a `map[string]HarnessAgentCoreMemoryRetrievalConfig` in the SDK, keyed by namespace path template. A map has no order, and the service returns one entry per memory strategy, so neither `SizeAtMost(1)` nor a list is a good fit for it.\\n* Ordering: the map-to-block-list flatten in `internal/framework/flex/autoflex_flatten.go` builds the target slice with `for _, key := range vFrom.MapKeys()` and no sort, so element order comes from Go's randomized map iteration rather than from anything stable. Any `map_block_key` block holding two or more entries can reorder between applies; this resource happens to expose it because the entries are service-populated.\\n* `memory_actual` was added in `#49383` (released in v6.60.0) and is not covered by `ignore_changes` on `memory`, which is why suppressing the count mismatch does not suppress the ordering mismatch.\\n* The acceptance tests for `memory.agentcore_memory_configuration` (`_basic`, `_options`, `_retrievalConfig`) all use an `aws_bedrockagentcore_memory` with no `aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy` attached, so no test exercises a memory that has strategies. This gap is not listed in `#49356` (the acceptance testing gaps issue for this resource), which enumerates `environment`, `model`, `skill`, `tool` and `truncation`.\\n* `#48159` is the same class of problem on the `environment` block (server-populated fields that were not declared `Computed`), fixed in `#48815` and reworked in `#49449`. The `memory` block was not touched by either.\\n* `#49374` reports the same shape on `aws_bedrockagentcore_gateway_target.metadata_configuration`: a value the service returns on create that cannot be declared in configuration.\\n* Possible directions: declare the `retrieval_config` attributes `Computed` (as was done for `environment`), sort by `map_block_key` when flattening a map into a block list (or model it as a map attribute), and reconsider `SizeAtMost(1)` given the service returns one entry per memory strategy.\\n\\n### Would you like to implement a fix?\\n\\nNo\\n\\n\",\n7\t  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n8\t  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n9\t  \"labels\": [],\n10\t  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:09:25Z\",\n11\t  \"as_of\": \"2026-08-20T04:06:35Z\"\n12\t}"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7196f91e-9532-49ae-afb7-cfcbe2e60a54","uuid":"9f5e46de-32fc-48d5-a7ac-17fb7a414887","timestamp":"2026-10-28T17:39:57.867Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/tmp/gh-aw/agent/issue.json","content":"{\n  \"number\": 28,\n  \"url\": \"https://github.com/wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent/issues/28\",\n  \"repo\": \"wzinl/cs467-as3-triage-agent\",\n  \"title\": \"[Bug]: aws_bedrockagentcore_harness: service-populated memory retrieval_config makes every apply fail with \\\"inconsistent result after apply\\\"\",\n  \"body\": \"### Terraform and AWS Provider Version\\n\\n```console\\nTerraform v1.14.3\\non darwin_arm64\\n+ provider registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws v6.60.0\\n```\\n\\n### Affected Resource(s) or Data Source(s)\\n\\n* `aws_bedrockagentcore_harness`\\n\\n### Expected Behavior\\n\\nWhen `memory.agentcore_memory_configuration` only references an existing AgentCore Memory by ARN and declares no `retrieval_config` block, apply should succeed and the following plan should be empty.\\n\\n### Actual Behavior\\n\\nEvery apply that creates or replaces the harness fails with `Provider produced inconsistent result after apply`, because the service returns one `retrieval_config` entry per memory strategy and the provider writes them into a block the configuration never declared:\\n\\n```\\n.memory[0].agentcore_memory_configuration[0].retrieval_config:\\nblock count changed from 0 to 2.\\n```\\n\\nThe harness is created in AWS, but the resource is left tainted, so the next apply replaces it and fails the same way. I hit this 5 times out of 5.\\n\\nTwo further points:\\n\\n1. `retrieval_config` carries `listvalidator.SizeAtMost(1)`, so the state that the provider itself writes (2 entries) could not be expressed in a configuration.\\n2. The order of the two entries is not stable across applies. In the SDK, `RetrievalConfig` is a `map[string]HarnessAgentCoreMemoryRetrievalConfig` keyed by namespace path template, so there is no order to preserve, but the schema models it as a list and the flatten path iterates `MapKeys()` without sorting. Watching the state across consecutive applies of an unchanged configuration:\\n\\n   | apply | `retrieval_config` order (`map_block_key` / strategy) |\\n   |---|---|\\n   | 1 | `/{sessionId}` (summarization), `default` (semantic) |\\n   | 2 | `/{sessionId}` (summarization), `default` (semantic) |\\n   | 3 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n   | 4 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n   | 5 | `default` (semantic), `/{sessionId}` (summarization) |\\n\\n   Because the block is modelled as a list, this ordering is part of the value being compared, so it can also surface as `.retrieval_config[0].strategy_id: was cty.StringVal(\\\"<semantic id>\\\"), but now cty.StringVal(\\\"<summarization id>\\\")` rather than as a block count change. We see that variant on harnesses that carry `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [memory] }`, where the count mismatch is suppressed but `memory_actual` still moves.\\n\\n### Relevant Error/Panic Output\\n\\n```console\\nError: Provider produced inconsistent result after apply\\n\\nWhen applying changes to aws_bedrockagentcore_harness.test, provider\\n\\\"provider[\\\\\\\"registry.terraform.io/hashicorp/aws\\\\\\\"]\\\" produced an unexpected\\nnew value: .memory[0].agentcore_memory_configuration[0].retrieval_config:\\nblock count changed from 0 to 2.\\n\\nThis is a bug in the provider, which should be reported in the provider's own\\nissue tracker.\\n```\\n\\n### Sample Terraform Configuration\\n\\n<details open>\\n<summary>Click to expand configuration</summary>\\n\\n```hcl\\nterraform {\\n  required_providers {\\n    aws = {\\n      source  = \\\"hashicorp/aws\\\"\\n      version = \\\"6.60.0\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nprovider \\\"aws\\\" {\\n  region = \\\"us-west-2\\\"\\n}\\n\\ndata \\\"aws_iam_policy_document\\\" \\\"assume_role\\\" {\\n  statement {\\n    effect  = \\\"Allow\\\"\\n    actions = [\\\"sts:AssumeRole\\\"]\\n\\n    principals {\\n      type        = \\\"Service\\\"\\n      identifiers = [\\\"bedrock-agentcore.amazonaws.com\\\"]\\n    }\\n  }\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_iam_role\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name               = \\\"harness-retrieval-config-repro\\\"\\n  assume_role_policy = data.aws_iam_policy_document.assume_role.json\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_iam_role_policy\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  role = aws_iam_role.test.name\\n\\n  policy = jsonencode({\\n    Version = \\\"2012-10-17\\\"\\n    Statement = [{\\n      Effect   = \\\"Allow\\\"\\n      Action   = [\\\"bedrock:InvokeModel\\\", \\\"bedrock:InvokeModelWithResponseStream\\\"]\\n      Resource = \\\"*\\\"\\n    }]\\n  })\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  name                  = \\\"harness_repro_mem\\\"\\n  event_expiry_duration = 7\\n}\\n\\n# Two strategies on the same memory. This is what makes the service return\\n# two retrieval configs to the harness.\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy\\\" \\\"semantic\\\" {\\n  name                = \\\"harness_repro_semantic\\\"\\n  memory_id           = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.id\\n  type                = \\\"SEMANTIC\\\"\\n  namespace_templates = [\\\"default\\\"]\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy\\\" \\\"summarization\\\" {\\n  name                = \\\"harness_repro_summary\\\"\\n  memory_id           = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.id\\n  type                = \\\"SUMMARIZATION\\\"\\n  namespace_templates = [\\\"/{sessionId}\\\"]\\n}\\n\\nresource \\\"aws_bedrockagentcore_harness\\\" \\\"test\\\" {\\n  harness_name       = \\\"harness_retrieval_config_repro\\\"\\n  execution_role_arn = aws_iam_role.test.arn\\n\\n  # No retrieval_config block: everything that ends up in state under\\n  # retrieval_config comes from the service.\\n  memory {\\n    agentcore_memory_configuration {\\n      arn = aws_bedrockagentcore_memory.test.arn\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  model {\\n    bedrock_model_config {\\n      model_id = \\\"anthropic.claude-sonnet-4-20250514\\\"\\n    }\\n  }\\n\\n  system_prompt {\\n    text = \\\"You are a helpful assistant.\\\"\\n  }\\n\\n  depends_on = [\\n    aws_iam_role_policy.test,\\n    aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy.semantic,\\n    aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy.summarization,\\n  ]\\n}\\n```\\n\\n</details>\\n\\n### Steps to Reproduce\\n\\n1. `terraform apply`. The harness is created in AWS, but the apply exits non-zero with the error above.\\n2. `terraform apply` again. The plan now says `is tainted, so must be replaced`, and the replacement fails the same way. Repeating this never converges.\\n3. `terraform show -json` after each attempt to observe the two `retrieval_config` entries the service added, and their changing order.\\n\\nThe state can be recovered with `terraform untaint aws_bedrockagentcore_harness.test` plus `lifecycle { ignore_changes = [memory] }`, after which plans are empty. That only hides the diff, and the first create still fails.\\n\\n### Debug Logging\\n\\nn/a. Happy to capture `TF_LOG=DEBUG` output if it would help.\\n\\n### GenAI / LLM Assisted Development\\n\\nClaude Code was used to research the provider source and to draft this report. The configuration above was applied against AWS, and the error output and the ordering table come from those runs.\\n\\n### Important Facts and References\\n\\n* Schema (`internal/service/bedrockagentcore/harness.go`): `retrieval_config` is a `ListNestedBlock` with `listvalidator.SizeAtMost(1)`, and none of `map_block_key`, `relevance_score`, `strategy_id`, `top_k` are `Computed`. The service fills all four (it returned `relevance_score = 0.2` and `top_k = 10` for both entries here), so with a memory that has two strategies the provider writes a value the schema does not allow to be written by hand.\\n* Underlying API shape: `HarnessAgentCoreMemoryConfiguration.RetrievalConfig` is a `map[string]HarnessAgentCoreMemoryRetrievalConfig` in the SDK, keyed by namespace path template. A map has no order, and the service returns one entry per memory strategy, so neither `SizeAtMost(1)` nor a list is a good fit for it.\\n* Ordering: the map-to-block-list flatten in `internal/framework/flex/autoflex_flatten.go` builds the target slice with `for _, key := range vFrom.MapKeys()` and no sort, so element order comes from Go's randomized map iteration rather than from anything stable. Any `map_block_key` block holding two or more entries can reorder between applies; this resource happens to expose it because the entries are service-populated.\\n* `memory_actual` was added in `#49383` (released in v6.60.0) and is not covered by `ignore_changes` on `memory`, which is why suppressing the count mismatch does not suppress the ordering mismatch.\\n* The acceptance tests for `memory.agentcore_memory_configuration` (`_basic`, `_options`, `_retrievalConfig`) all use an `aws_bedrockagentcore_memory` with no `aws_bedrockagentcore_memory_strategy` attached, so no test exercises a memory that has strategies. This gap is not listed in `#49356` (the acceptance testing gaps issue for this resource), which enumerates `environment`, `model`, `skill`, `tool` and `truncation`.\\n* `#48159` is the same class of problem on the `environment` block (server-populated fields that were not declared `Computed`), fixed in `#48815` and reworked in `#49449`. The `memory` block was not touched by either.\\n* `#49374` reports the same shape on `aws_bedrockagentcore_gateway_target.metadata_configuration`: a value the service returns on create that cannot be declared in configuration.\\n* Possible directions: declare the `retrieval_config` attributes `Computed` (as was done for `environment`), sort by `map_block_key` when flattening a map into a block list (or model it as a map attribute), and reconsider `SizeAtMost(1)` given the service returns one entry per memory strategy.\\n\\n### Would you like to implement a fix?\\n\\nNo\\n\\n\",\n  \"author\": \"zzzinn\",\n  \"author_association\": \"NONE\",\n  \"labels\": [],\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-28T17:09:25Z\",\n  \"as_of\": \"2026-08-20T04:06:35Z\"\n}","numLines":12,"startLine":1,"totalLines":12}},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01MBwrnN6s5cXHgrM3d3b8Yn","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"rule"}}]}
